[с. 7]
plastid versus nuclear incongruence at various levels across
angiosperms. In addition to the discordance between
organellar and nuclear trees, studies have also highlighted
conﬂicting relationships identiﬁed with multiple nuclear
genes (e.g., Escobar et al., 2011; Som, 2015; Sun et al., 2015).
Although noise, error, and paralogy probably contribute to
some discordance between genes in the nuclear genomes,
there has also been increasing support for biological causes
of incongruence, including ILS, gene ﬂow via hybridization/
introgression, extensive rate heterogeneity, and possibly
others. Genomic data have revealed multiple instances of
deep incongruence in the angiosperms (Stull et al., 2023;
Zuntini et al., 2024), although few of these phenomena aﬀect
the classiﬁcation of families and orders, which are the focus
of APG. The position of Ericales has been unresolved due to
them likely being a product of ancient hybridization between
ancestors of Cornales and the larger asterid clade (c. 120 Ma;
Stull et al., 2020). Likewise, Guo et al. (2021) found evidence
that ancient hybridization could account for the incongruent
phylogenetic placement of Chloranthales and magnoliids in
nuclear and plastid analyses (Table 1).
3.5 Plastid and nuclear trees
In APG V, we base alterations of APG IV (2016) on wellsupported nuclear results, particularly if also supported by
large analyses of plastome data (Li et al., 2021). The results
on which circumscription of APG V orders and families are
based have been generally consistent across phylogenetic
methods and assumptions, and are parallel in coalescent,
concatenated (combined), and most forms of weighted
analyses. If extensive nuclear gene‐tree incongruence has
been detected, such that the position of a group is
unresolved or only weakly supported, then we indicate
uncertainty of result by considering them as “unplaced” to
order or major clade (e.g., fabids, asterids). Likewise, if a set

851

of relationships varies depending on the method used, we
also indicate this as a polytomy for the groups concerned,
unless noted in the section below on speciﬁc problematic
parts of APG V (e.g., placement of Dasypogonaceae in
Arecales, which has considerable gene‐tree discordance at
that node, that is, no portion of identical gene trees greater
than 50% or, out of three possible quartet topologies, no
more than two above one‐third). Positions of some taxa in
some plastid analyses should simply be discarded if they
conﬂict with well‐supported relationships from analyses of
multiple nuclear genes. From an evolutionary standpoint,
plastid trees are informative of, for example, the parentage
of hybrids and phylogeographic population structure.
However, if there is evidence of considerable nuclear genetree discordance (some of it perhaps agreeing with plastid
results, but no congruent result in greater than 50% of
nuclear gene trees) and/or if the source of discordance
remains unclear, then, we propose to treat relationships as
uncertain/preliminary, showing them as polytomies in the
APG V summary tree (Fig. 1). We provide a summary of some
examples (Table 1) and indicate the source of incongruence,
if known. Many require further investigation before the
source(s) of discordance can be clariﬁed.
3.6 Reproducibility
Reproducibility in a classiﬁcation is a desirable attribute (i.e.,
diﬀerent authors reaching the same ideas of taxon delimitation
based on the same data), but the idiosyncratic nature of plant
classiﬁcation makes this a diﬃcult goal to achieve. The initial
APG classiﬁcation (1998) did not start with a clean slate, and all
iterations of APG have been mixtures of historical concepts
and new ideas about relationships. Many small family pairs, for
example, Ancistrocladaceae–Dioncophyllaceae, Aextoxicaceae–
Berberidopsidaceae, Curtisiaceae–Grubbiaceae, Asteropeiaceae–
Physenaceae, and others, have been considered for merger in

