Acknowledgements
We thank the hundreds of people who commented on an
earlier draft of the manuscript and classiﬁcation via an on‐line
survey. We received 234 responses: 33% from Europe, 26%
from Asia, 23% from South America, 13% from North America,
3% from Australasia/Oceania, and 3% from Africa. Respondents
were associated with a university (62%), herbarium (32%),
botanical garden (30%), or museum (8%) (note that these
categories were not mutually exclusive). Overall, 62% of the
respondents were more than 80% in agreement with the APG
V classiﬁcation, 14% agreed with 50%–80%, and only 7% agreed
with less than 50%; 17% had no opinion. Most of the
disagreements were focused on Boraginales and Gesneriaceae/Calceolariaceae/Peltantheraceae. Both issues are discussed at length above. We gratefully acknowledge a grant
from the Calleva Foundation to the Royal Botanic Gardens,
Kew, for supporting an in‐person meeting in October 2022, at
which the substance of this update was discussed.

Authors Contributions
Mark W. Chase, Pamela S. Soltis, and Douglas E. Soltis led the
writing with major contributions from James W. Byng,
Maarten J. M. Christenhusz, Michael F. Fay, Peter F. Stevens,
David J. Mabberley, De‐Zhu Li, and Hong Ma. Contributions
on their areas of speciality and/or advance information on
upcoming publications were provided by William J. Baker,
Steven Dodsworth, Felix Forest, Olivier Maurin, Lisa Pokorny,
Stephen A. Smith, and Alexandre. R. Zuntini. James W. Byng
created Figure 1.

Conﬂicts of Interest
The authors declare no conﬂict of interest.

